  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The key-escrow suite provisions from the script the package SHIPS, not from a copy. These
      three tables previously had no shipped DDL at all while every suite passed on its own
      hand-written CREATE TABLE, so nothing covered the file a consumer actually runs. Linking it
      in means a column added to the product's schema reaches the tests automatically, and one
      that is missing fails them.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Compliance.SqlServer\Scripts\002_CreateKeyEscrowSchema.sql" Link="Schema\002_CreateKeyEscrowSchema.sql" />
    <!--
      The erasure/legal-hold tenant-totality suites provision AND migrate from the scripts the
      packages ship, for the same reason. The two dialects both ship a file called
      001_CreateComplianceSchema.sql, so each is linked under a dialect-named folder: the resource
      name is derived from the Link path, and a shared leaf would make the two indistinguishable to
      a suffix match — a suite would silently load the other engine's DDL.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Compliance.SqlServer\Scripts\001_CreateComplianceSchema.sql" Link="Schema\SqlServer\001_CreateComplianceSchema.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Compliance.SqlServer\Scripts\003_MakeComplianceTenantTotal.sql" Link="Schema\SqlServer\003_MakeComplianceTenantTotal.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Compliance.Postgres\Scripts\001_CreateComplianceSchema.sql" Link="Schema\Postgres\001_CreateComplianceSchema.sql" />
    <!-- Dead-letter DDL, linked under the same dialect-named folders and for the same reason: both
         engines ship a file named 001_CreateDeadLetterSchema.sql, and neither dead-letter store creates
         its table at runtime, so the conformance suites must provision from the shipped script. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Data.Postgres\Scripts\001_CreateDeadLetterSchema.sql" Link="Schema\Postgres\001_CreateDeadLetterSchema.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Data.SqlServer\Scripts\001_CreateDeadLetterSchema.sql" Link="Schema\SqlServer\001_CreateDeadLetterSchema.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Compliance.Postgres\Scripts\002_MakeComplianceTenantTotal.sql" Link="Schema\Postgres\002_MakeComplianceTenantTotal.sql" />
    <!--
      The data-inventory tenant-totality migrations. These carry more weight than the two above,
      because the shape they migrate FROM is the one every existing deployment holds: the tenant
      discriminator reached the inventory tables by editing 001 in place, and both provisioning
      paths guard on table existence, so an upgrading consumer's tables are never reshaped. Linking
      these in is what lets a suite prove the migration actually repairs that database rather than
      asserting it against a schema only a fresh install has.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Compliance.SqlServer\Scripts\004_MakeDataInventoryTenantTotal.sql" Link="Schema\SqlServer\004_MakeDataInventoryTenantTotal.sql" />
    <!--
      The index-width repair that follows 004 in the real upgrade chain. It is embedded because the
      legacy-regress fixture has to be able to put the shared inventory tables BACK the way it found
      them: 001 now creates the shape 004 and 006 together leave behind, so a suite that regresses
      past both must replay both, or every later suite in the collection meets a schema no consumer
      has. Not embedding it is also what let the fixture drift behind this script in the first place.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Compliance.SqlServer\Scripts\006_MakeInventoryKeysFitTheIndexLimit.sql" Link="Schema\SqlServer\006_MakeInventoryKeysFitTheIndexLimit.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Compliance.Postgres\Scripts\003_MakeDataInventoryTenantTotal.sql" Link="Schema\Postgres\003_MakeDataInventoryTenantTotal.sql" />
    <!--
      The key-escrow tenant-totality migration. This one is bound not because its shape is
      interesting but because its RESTRAINT is: the stored term is AEAD associated data, so the
      migration is required NOT to backfill the sentinel, and the arm that binds it proves a key
      escrowed under the older shape still recovers after the shipped script has run. A future edit
      that "finishes the job" with an UPDATE would satisfy every schema check and destroy the key
      material; this is what makes that edit fail here first.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\..\src\Excalibur\Excalibur.Compliance.SqlServer\Scripts\005_MakeEscrowTenantTotal.sql" Link="Schema\SqlServer\005_MakeEscrowTenantTotal.sql" />
  </ItemGroup>
